++
++## Analysis: Why Time Basis Mapping Selected "as_et" Mode
++
++### Expected Behavior (if sim were correct)
++1. PDF checkpoints use absolute time (includes 0.131s rollout offset for QuarterProTest1)
++2. Sim should produce ET times starting from 0 at rollout
++3. Mode B (abs_minus_offset) should have lower error
++
++### Actual Behavior
++1. PDF @ 330ft: 2.84s (absolute time)
++2. Expected sim @ 330ft: 2.84 - 0.131 = **2.71s** (ET basis)
++3. Actual sim @ 330ft: **4.01s**
++4. Mode A error: |4.01 - 2.84| = 1.17s
++5. Mode B error: |4.01 - 2.71| = 1.30s
++6. **Mode A selected** (lower error)
++
++### Conclusion
++The sim is producing times that are **1.3s slower** than expected at 330ft. This is a **physics accuracy issue**, not a time basis issue. The time mapping correctly identifies that Mode A (treating PDF times as-is) produces lower error because the sim is fundamentally slow.
++
++---
++
++## Root Cause: Physics Accuracy Gap
++
++### Evidence
++1. **Deltas increase with distance** - Not characteristic of time basis misalignment
++   - 60-330ft: 1.1s mean
++   - 330-660ft: 2.0s mean
++   - 660-1000ft: 2.8s mean
++   
++2. **Both ET and MPH are wrong** - Time basis issues would only affect ET
++   - ET deltas: 1-10s
++   - MPH deltas: 17-48mph
++
++3. **Pattern consistent across all models**
++   - QuarterJr: Similar large deltas
++   - QuarterPro: 1-10s slower
++   - BonnevillePro: Similar pattern
++
++### Possible Causes
++1. **Launch/traction model differences** - Sim may be using different traction coefficients
++2. **Power application** - HP curve interpolation or application may differ from VB6
++3. **Drivetrain efficiency** - Losses may be calculated differently
++4. **Timestep/integration** - Numerical integration differences
++5. **Aerodynamic drag** - Drag model may be more conservative
++
++---
++
