++
++## 🔍 Root Cause Analysis
++
++The existing classic parity test infrastructure was designed for **minimal PDF-extracted data** (only 6-7 fields extracted from PDFs). It uses a normalization step that:
++
++1. Takes `ClassicRawInputs` (now 60+ fields)
++2. Applies VB6-style IF logic to compute 6 "effective" values
++3. **Discards everything else**
++4. Passes only those 6 values to `classic_sim_runner`
++
++This works for PDF parity tests because PDFs only had 6-7 fields to begin with. But for JSON cases with **full vehicle specifications**, this causes massive field loss.
++
++---
++
++## 🎯 Solution Options
++
++### Option 1: Extend ClassicEffectiveInputs (Recommended)
++**Pros:**
++- Preserves existing normalization logic
++- Maintains VB6-style IF branching for defaults
++- Backward compatible with PDF parity tests
++
++**Cons:**
++- Requires updating `ClassicEffectiveInputs` type
++- Requires updating `normalizeClassicCase()` to preserve fields
++- Requires updating `classic_sim_runner` to use extended inputs
++
++**Implementation:**
++```typescript
++export interface ClassicEffectiveInputs {
++  // Existing fields
++  weightLb: number;
++  powerHP_effective: number;
++  tireDiaIn_effective: number;
++  rearGear_effective: number;
++  rolloutIn_effective: number | null;
++  engineCurve?: Array<{ rpm: number; hp: number; tq?: number }>;
++  finishDistFt_effective: number;
++  
++  // NEW: Preserve additional fields
++  wheelbaseIn?: number;
++  overhangIn?: number;
++  tireWidthIn?: number;
++  drivetrainEfficiency?: number;
++  drivetrainMode?: 'torqueConverter' | 'centrifugalClutch';
++  transmissionGearing?: Array<{ gear: number; ratio: number; efficiency?: number; shiftRpm?: number }>;
++  stallRpm?: number;
++  slipRpm?: number;
++  clutchSlippage?: number;
++  lockupOption?: boolean;
++  frontalArea_ft2?: number;
++  dragCoeff?: number;
++  liftCoeff?: number;
++  engineFlywheelClutchPMI?: number;
++  transmissionDriveshaftPMI?: number;
++  tiresWheelsRingGearPMI?: number;
++  elevation?: number;
++  barometerInHg?: number;
++  temperatureF?: number;
++  humidityPct?: number;
++  tractionIndex?: number;
++  windVelocityMph?: number;
++  windAngleDeg?: number;
++  trackTempF?: number;
++}
++```
++
++### Option 2: Bypass Normalization for JSON Cases
++**Pros:**
++- Quick fix
++- Doesn't affect existing PDF parity tests
++
++**Cons:**
++- Loses VB6-style IF logic and defaulting
++- Creates two separate code paths
++- May miss edge cases that normalization handles
++
++### Option 3: Create JSON-Specific Simulation Runner
++**Pros:**
++- Clean separation of concerns
++- Can optimize for JSON format
++
++**Cons:**
++- Code duplication
++- Harder to maintain consistency
