++## TECHNICAL LEARNINGS
++
++### 1. VB6 Rounding is Banker's Rounding
++VB6's `Round()` function uses "round half to even" (banker's rounding), not "round half up" like JavaScript's `Math.round()`. This matters for values exactly at 0.5.
++
++**Example:**
++- 20.5 → 20 (rounds to even)
++- 21.5 → 22 (rounds to even)
++
++**Implementation:**
++```typescript
++if (Math.abs(frac - 0.5) < 0.0001) {
++  const floor = Math.floor(dist_ft);
++  const result = floor % 2 === 0 ? floor : floor + 1;
++  return `${result}ft`;
++}
++```
++
++### 2. Closest-Match Fallbacks Hide Real Issues
++The previous implementation used a 2ft tolerance to match checkpoints, which papered over the physics parity gap. Removing this fallback exposed the real issue.
++
++**Lesson:** Don't use tolerance-based matching in equivalence tests - it hides bugs.
++
++### 3. Physics Parity is Harder Than Logic Parity
++Matching VB6's emitter logic is straightforward (triggers, interpolation, formatting). Matching VB6's physics simulation is much harder due to:
++- Adaptive timesteps
++- Floating point precision
++- Complex force calculations
++- Initial condition sensitivity
++
++**Lesson:** Separate "emitter correctness" from "physics correctness" in acceptance criteria.
