++## Conclusion: Summary MPH is 66-ft Trap Speed
++
++**Answer:** QuarterPro summary MPH is **66-ft trap speed**, NOT instantaneous velocity.
++
++**Formula:** `mph = 45.0 / dt` where:
++- `dt = t_finish - t_save` (time to travel last 66 ft)
++- `45.0 = 66 * 3600 / 5280` (exact constant for ft/s to mph conversion over 66 ft)
++- For EIGHTH mile: `t_save` at 594 ft, `t_finish` at 660 ft
++- For QUARTER mile: `t_save` at 1254 ft, `t_finish` at 1320 ft
++
++**Float32 Precision:** VB6 uses Single (Float32) for:
++1. `saveTime` when recorded at 594/1254 ft
++2. `interpolatedTrackTime` when computing trap MPH
++3. `dt` (the time difference)
++4. Final `mph` result
++
++**Storage:** The computed trap MPH is stored in `timeslip[].v_mph` at the 660/1320 ft entries.
++
++**Special Case:** Bonneville (land speed) uses instantaneous velocity instead of trap speed (line 1406, 1411 comments).
